Z||000013||artherd||12-11-2001||09:15 PM||||Not their motor oil, but I have Red Line MTL in the Trannys, and I use their synthetic chassis grease exclusively.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mark Matthews:<BR><B>So does anybody use RedLine Products?  Or am I the only one?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000014||Mark Matthews||12-11-2001||09:25 PM||||Luckily theres a shop here in town that keeps it all in stock.  I was using Mobil 1, till I found the shop that sells RedLine.<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||12-13-2001||12:44 AM||||Have you had any expierence with the RedLine out performing Mobil 1 ?<P>Mobil 1 has a near perfect reputation, runs great, even has a MIL part number!<P>Both products seem very good, but I wouldn't say either was 'better' in any lubricating way.<P>That just leaves booring things like cost and availability. (there's a shop locally with Redline too, and I'll gladly order anything that my cars will like anyway, but hey.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mark Matthews:<BR><B>Luckily theres a shop here in town that keeps it all in stock.  I was using Mobil 1, till I found the shop that sells RedLine.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000016||Mark Matthews||12-13-2001||09:23 AM||||I like RedLine.  It definetly made a difference in my 30 year old BMW, but that's a different story.  I haven't had the Fiero long enough to compare the two, about a week.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I wouldn't use anything but Redline in my diff's and tranny's.  Nor any of those other fuel additive or oil additive things, what crap.  There was a report, if I can find it I'll post it, that should most big name additives actually harm the engine!  RedLine wasn't one of them, it is the only product line I'd swear by.  No I don't work for them   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>If you plan on having your car for a while, I'd suggest finding a local retailer that carries it.<P>I'll get down off my soapbox now.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top||||reg||14
Z||000017||terryk||12-13-2001||06:10 PM||||The only downside I've ever heard about Mobil 1 (and I use it in all of my cars) is that it tends to completely drip off all parts in the engine over an extended period of time (like the main and rod bearings). <P>I know several people that store GN/TR's over the winter and they always prime the engine first. I guess a couple of people tore up the cranks on the first start of spring. Might be related to the lousy GN/TR oil pumps too. <P>Minor detail compared to the benefits.||||reg||1
Z||000018||theogre||12-13-2001||08:37 PM||||I think, the Tri Synth labeled replaces the older labeled. If you are still finding the older label on shelves then it's likely old stock.<P>I run Mobil 1. I'll never go back unless I absolutly have to. I started using it at around 150,000 miles. been using it for over a year now with no problems at all from the change.<P>Terry, I'd prime any motor after sitting all winter regardless of what oil is in there. No oil is going to stay in place for that long. The Mobil 1 might be a little worse than crude for that but they will all leave the motor to dry after sitting for months.<P>Can someone tell me what months Walmart usually does the 5 quart Mobil 1 deal? I almost never seem to be able to catch it.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||RossT||12-14-2001||01:11 AM||||Walmart has it in 5 quart jugs twice a year for $17.88  I normally buy a years worth at the end of July. I am unsure what winter month they have it..||||reg||1
Z||000020||fierospeeder||12-14-2001||03:42 AM||||i do the same as theorge<P>if my fiero has been sitting for a long time.  I pull the plug on my ign coil, and let the starter crank the engine till the oil light goes off.  I put the plug back on, and start the car.<P><BR>I love mobil 1, too bad its expensive.  I always go to my walmart too and never ever seen a 5 gallon jug besides the newspaper ad.<P>------------------<BR>Fiero Technician<P>74 Vw Beetle<BR>83 Honda Nighthawk 550<BR>84 Pontiac Fiero<BR>87 Ford Thunderbird Turbo<P>Selling a car is like giving your own kid for adoption.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Eriamjh||12-14-2001||01:04 PM||||I always use regular oil for winter storage.  Since it sits the whole time, why pay for synthetic?<P>After a warm up in the spring, change it out again and put in the Mobil 1.  Saves a bit of money (unless you don't change oil in the spring).||||reg||1
Z||000022||REDHOTT88||12-14-2001||01:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Eriamjh:<BR><B>I always use regular oil for winter storage.  Since it sits the whole time, why pay for synthetic?<P>After a warm up in the spring, change it out again and put in the Mobil 1.  Saves a bit of money (unless you don't change oil in the spring).<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Yeah, but the conventional has additives, chemicals, acids, gums, why have all that sitting in your engine all winter? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||GTDude||12-17-2001||09:17 AM||||Winter Storage!!!!!!!!!?????????  I drive my Fiero year round.  I love it too much to put it away for the winter.  Of course, I live in Virginia where we don't get alot of snow or bad weather.....so I can see the point if you live in very cold climates that have alot of snow and road chemicals.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||12
